  "worked_solution": "The period of $y=sec\u2061x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\nsec\u2061x=sec\u2061(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=4$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec\u2061x=sec\u2061(x+2\\cdot 4\\cdot \\pi )=sec\u2061(x+8\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $sec\u2061x=3\\sqrt{3},$ then $sec\u2061(x+8\\pi )=3\\sqrt{3}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
